## Prerequisites

This plan must execute AFTER the sibling plan at
`src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands/plan.aide`, because `scaffoldCommands`
ships with a `COMMANDS` registry that still names
`{ canonical: "commands/aide/init", hostPath: "aide/init.md", ... }`. As
long as that entry is present, deleting `.claude/commands/aide/init.md`
breaks two contracts simultaneously: (a) any subsequent `npx aidemd-mcp init`
or `aide_init` invocation will surface a `would-skip` step trying to read
a canonical doc that no longer exists; (b) `initContent`'s `DOC_PATHS`
registry still points at the missing file and will throw on lookup. The
contract this plan consumes from the prerequisite is therefore:

- **`src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands` (sibling plan)** must remove the
  `commands/aide/init` entry from the `COMMANDS` registry in
  `src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands/index.ts` AND the matching
  `"commands/aide/init"` entry from the `DOC_PATHS` registry in
  `src/tools/init/initContent/index.ts`. After both removals, the
  `aide_init` tool's planning pass produces twelve scaffold steps (one
  orchestrator + eleven subcommands) and never attempts to read
  `.claude/commands/aide/init.md`. Only after that registry change
  lands is it safe for this plan's step 2 to delete the file.

No other prerequisites. The orchestrator-prompt edits in step 1 are
internally self-contained — `aide.md` has no compile-time link to the
deleted file beyond the `/aide:init` text references this plan rewrites.

## Plan

### 1. Rewrite the three `/aide:init` references in `.claude/commands/aide.md`

Read:
- `coding-playbook/agentic/command-modules/multi-agent-pipelines` — orchestrator prompt discipline, no surprise routing changes, file-based-handoff invariants
- `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions` — prose clarity, no commented-out content, naming rules

- [x] Open `.claude/commands/aide.md` and locate every `/aide:init` token.
  At the time this plan was written there are exactly three reference
  sites. Confirm by re-reading the file before editing — drift would
  invalidate the rewrite.

  Reference site A — the `no-mcp-entry` branch (currently ~line 32-38).
  The branch starts with `**\`status === 'no-mcp-entry'\`** — Stop. Tell
  the user:` and contains a blockquote with the `/aide:init` instruction.
  This is a cold-start case: the user has no obsidian MCP entry at all.
  The cli (`npx aidemd-mcp init`) writes that entry as a side effect of
  its mcp step. So the replacement is `npx aidemd-mcp init`. The full
  blockquote rewrites to (preserve the existing tone, blockquote shape,
  and the surrounding `Do NOT proceed with any pipeline work until this is resolved.` line):

  > One step left before the pipeline can run: wiring up an obsidian MCP
  > entry so AIDE has a brain vault to persist research and retros into.
  >
  > Run `npx aidemd-mcp init` in this project's terminal — it'll add the
  > obsidian MCP entry to `.mcp.json` and tell you what to do next. Once
  > that's done, restart Claude Code and re-run `/aide`.

  The "restart Claude Code" sentence is load-bearing: `.mcp.json` is
  loaded at Claude Code boot, so a same-session re-run of `/aide` after
  the cli completes would still see the empty in-process MCP. Mirror
  the inline-recovery flow's restart instruction in tone.

  Reference site B — the failure fallback inside the empty-`vaultPath`
  inline-recovery flow (currently in step 6, ~lines 76 and 80). Step 6
  catches three failure modes (mcp call throws, .mcp.json merge throws,
  brain call throws) and currently routes the user to `/aide:init`. With
  `/aide:init` gone, the fallback must route to `npx aidemd-mcp init`
  (cold-start retry from the cli) instead. The failure blockquote
  rewrites to:

  > Something went wrong wiring the vault path: `<error message>`.
  >
  > Run `npx aidemd-mcp init` in this project's terminal to retry the
  > setup with full per-file logging, then restart Claude Code and
  > re-run `/aide`.

  Keep the surrounding sentences ("Do NOT retry inline. Do NOT re-prompt
  the user. Surface the specific error and fall back to ...") intact;
  only the trailing command name and blockquote change. Drop the existing
  reference to `/aide:init` in the surrounding sentence — say "fall back
  to the cli" or restructure the sentence so the prose doesn't dangle.

  Reference site C — the non-empty-but-broken `vaultPath` branch
  (currently ~line 82-87). The user has an obsidian MCP entry that
  points at a path that doesn't resolve on disk. The current copy says
  "Either restore the folder at that path, or run `/aide:init` to pick
  a different one." The replacement is `npx aidemd-mcp init` — the cli
  re-prompts for `--vault-path` (or, when run without it, leaves the
  shell empty and triggers the orchestrator's inline-recovery flow on
  the next `/aide`). The full blockquote rewrites to:

  > The obsidian MCP entry points to `<brain.vaultPath>`, but that
  > directory isn't there.
  >
  > Either restore the folder at that path, or run
  > `npx aidemd-mcp init --vault-path <new-path>` in this project's
  > terminal to point at a different one. Once the path resolves,
  > restart Claude Code and `/aide` will continue.

  The `<brain.vaultPath>` token is a template variable that the
  orchestrator substitutes at runtime — it is preserved as-is.

- [x] Verify after the three rewrites that the file contains zero
  remaining occurrences of the literal string `/aide:init`. Run a
  search across the rewritten file as a sanity check — the failure
  fallback in step 6 currently has TWO occurrences in adjacent lines
  (one in the surrounding prose, one inside the blockquote), so a
  single rewrite that misses the surrounding sentence is the most
  likely failure mode.

- [x] Do NOT touch any other content in `.claude/commands/aide.md`.
  The mandatory boot sequence, the inline-recovery flow's mcp+brain
  two-call pattern, the hard constraints, the routing-explicit-intent
  rules, the resume protocol, the pipeline stages, the refactor flow,
