## Decisions

- **The cold-start cli is the only correct replacement for the
  `no-mcp-entry` and non-empty-broken-`vaultPath` branches.**
  `/aide:upgrade` was rejected for both because upgrade is a drift
  operation (compare local AIDE artifacts against the canonical
  manifest and re-sync) — it does NOT touch `.mcp.json` and has no
  vault-path-resolution machinery. The cli already owns both
  responsibilities (mcp entry merge, optional `--vault-path`), and
  the orchestrator's job is to point the user at the right tool.

- **The orchestrator's inline-recovery flow is preserved verbatim.**
  Steps 1-5 of the empty-`vaultPath` branch already implement the
  two-call pattern (`aide_init({ category: "mcp" })` to patch
  `.mcp.json`, then `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` to seed the
  vault) and emit a restart instruction. None of that changes — only
  the failure fallback in step 6 is rewritten to point at the cli
  instead of `/aide:init`. The `aide_init` MCP tool and its
  sub-helpers (`provisionBrain`, `configureIde`, `installAgents`,
  etc.) stay exactly as they are. This matches the user's brief that
  the change is markdown-deletion + reference-update only.

- **Failure fallback redirects to `npx aidemd-mcp init`, not
  `/aide:init`.** Even though the failure happens INSIDE the
  orchestrator's inline-recovery flow, the right escape hatch is the
  cli, because the cli has full per-file logging and the
  orchestrator's inline branch deliberately omits that for the
  one-question-recovery happy path. The failure case is the moment
  the user needs the cli's verbose output, not a redundant slash
  command.

- **No orchestrator-side helper extraction.** A prompt file is prose
  instructing the orchestrator agent; it does not decompose into
  helper files the way executable code does. The orchestrator/helper
  pattern applies to `src/` code, not to `.claude/commands/aide.md`.
  The single file remains the right unit. (Same decision as the
  prior plan in this scope.)

- **The deleted `init.md` does NOT get a tombstone, redirect note, or
  archived copy.** Per the conventions playbook, deleted code is just
  deleted — git history preserves it. The next `aide_info` call
  reports the file as missing in any host project that already
  scaffolded it, and the next `/aide:upgrade` run will detect the
  drift through the artifact manifest comparison and offer to remove
  it. (Confirm the upgrade flow's deletion handling in a separate
  pass; if upgrade only handles add/update and not removal, that is
  a follow-up plan, not this one.)

- **Single-module scoping respected.** This plan governs only the
  `.claude/` harness. The sibling plan at
  `src/tools/init/scaffoldCommands/plan.aide` handles the registry
  removal and spec body updates. The README.md edits, the JSDoc
  updates in `src/types/index.ts`, `src/tools/info/index.ts`,
  `src/tools/info/buildBrainState/index.ts`, and
  `src/tools/init/provisionBrain/index.ts`, the four `.aide` specs
  in `src/cli/init/`, `src/tools/info/`, and `src/tools/init/`, and
  the cli's deferred-categories warning text in
  `src/cli/init/renderWarning/index.ts` (plus its tests and the
  `cli/init/index.ts` deferred-categories list) are all out of scope
  here. They are flagged in Risks below for separate plans.

- **Helpers' current location preserved per user's deferred-refactor
  decision.** The `aide_init` MCP tool's helpers (`provisionBrain/`,
  `configureIde/`, `installAgents/`, `installSkills/`,
  `installAideTree/`, `installMethodologyDocs/`, `scaffoldCommands/`,
  etc.) live under `src/tools/init/`. The user has explicitly
  deferred a services-layer refactor that would extract them into a
  shared services package consumed by both the MCP tool and the cli.
  This plan does NOT preemptively touch that structure; the
  deletion stays minimal and contained.

- **Why this plan executes AFTER the scaffoldCommands plan.** The
  ordering is forced by the `initContent.DOC_PATHS` registry: as
  long as `"commands/aide/init"` resolves to
  `.claude/commands/aide/init.md`, deleting the file will cause
  `readCanonicalDoc("commands/aide/init")` to throw at the next
  `aide_init` invocation. The sibling plan removes both the
  `COMMANDS` entry and the `DOC_PATHS` entry; only after that does
  it become safe to delete the file. The orchestrator (which
  executes plans in dependency order) must run the
  `scaffoldCommands` plan first.

## Risks

- **Out-of-scope `/aide:init` references remain across the codebase.**
  After this plan and its sibling execute, the literal string
  `/aide:init` will still appear in: `README.md` (two occurrences in
  the Quick Start section), JSDoc in `src/types/index.ts` (line 352),
  `src/tools/info/index.ts` (line 42), `src/tools/info/buildBrainState/index.ts`
  (lines 21 and 34), `src/tools/init/provisionBrain/index.ts`
  (line 44), and runtime user-facing text in
  `src/cli/init/renderWarning/index.ts` (lines 62 and 70) plus its
  tests. The `src/cli/init/.aide` spec also references `/aide:init`
  pervasively in its desired/undesired outcomes — that spec needs a
  full rewrite once the cli's deferred-categories messaging is
  re-aimed. None of these are blockers for the deprecation, but they
  produce incorrect remediation hints in the cli's warning block and
  in tool-facing JSDoc until they are cleaned up. **Surface these to
  the user before approving the build.** The cli warning text is the
  most user-visible: a future cold-start cli run will print
  "Open Claude Code in this project and run /aide:init to finish
  setup." even though `/aide:init` no longer exists. A separate cli
  plan must update `renderWarning` to point at the orchestrator's
  inline-recovery flow ("Open Claude Code and run `/aide` — it will
  prompt for a vault path and finish setup.") and update the
  deferred-categories list in `cli/init/index.ts` to drop the
  `/aide:init` references.

- **The orchestrator's inline-recovery flow now carries the entire
  user-facing burden of vault-path resolution.** Before this
  deletion, the user had two paths: the orchestrator's inline
  recovery (one-question, no per-file logging) and `/aide:init`
  (full wizard with hint surfacing, per-file logging, IDE prompts,
  framework detection). After the deletion, the inline recovery is
  the only interactive path inside Claude Code, and the cli is the
  only path with per-file logging. If the inline recovery breaks
  silently in production (the failure fallback rewrite in step 1
  routes to the cli, but only catches the three named failure modes
  in step 6 — anything beyond that surfaces as a generic agent
  error), the user has no in-Claude-Code recovery path. Watch for
  inline-recovery failure modes that the existing six-step flow does
  not handle.

- **Existing host projects already have `init.md` on disk.** Any
  project that ran `aide_init` before this deprecation has
  `.claude/commands/aide/init.md` scaffolded into its command
  directory. After they pull the new server version, the registry
  will no longer list the file, but the host file persists until
  `/aide:upgrade` removes it. Confirm during build whether
  `/aide:upgrade` actually deletes orphaned scaffolded files or
  only updates / adds — if it only does add/update, host projects
  will keep an orphan `init.md` until a future cleanup. The orphan
  is harmless (Claude Code will still register `/aide:init` as a
  slash command, but invoking it on a host project running a
  newer server will produce a wizard prompt that no longer
  reflects the canonical workflow). Recommend running the upgrade
  in a smoke-test host project after publish to confirm the
  deletion-handling behavior.

- **The prerequisite ordering is mechanical, not enforced by tooling.**
  If the orchestrator runs this plan's step 2 before the
  scaffoldCommands plan removes the `DOC_PATHS` entry, the next
  `aide_init` call in this project will throw on
  `readCanonicalDoc("commands/aide/init")`. Step 2 includes a
  verification gate that re-reads both registries before deleting,
  but the implementor must honor that gate.
